{# Arc 21-B: the prepared-order block + the LOG-ONLY intent form. ASCII only
   (no glyphs / em-dash). NO logic here -- every value is display-ready on
   `row.prepared_order`.

   THE WITHHELD BRANCH IS THE DEFAULT, NOT THE EXCEPTION. On today's substrate
   the regime is undeterminable on every card, so the withheld branch is what
   renders; it states its reason and offers NO control. There is deliberately
   NO disabled-looking ACCEPT button there -- a disabled-looking button that
   posts is worse than no button, and a test asserts the ACCEPT control is
   ABSENT FROM THE DOM in every withheld case.

   THE FORM CARRIES ITS OWN `hx-target="this"`. `hx-target` INHERITS from
   ancestors, and this card sits inside `.latch-cards`, so without it a future
   ancestor target would capture the swap and land the 200 fragment somewhere
   unrelated. `hx-headers` with HX-Request is what keeps OriginGuard strict
   mode from 403-ing an unsafe method. #}
{% set po = row.prepared_order %}
{% if po %}
<section class="latch-prepared-order latch-prepared-order-{{ 'offered' if po.offered else 'withheld' }}">
  {% if po.offered %}
  <h3 class="latch-prepared-order-heading">{{ po.log_only_notice }}</h3>
  <p class="latch-prepared-order-headline">{{ po.headline }}</p>
  <pre class="latch-derivation">{% for line in po.derivation_lines %}{{ line }}
{% endfor %}</pre>
  <form class="latch-intent-form" hx-post="/latches/intent"
        hx-target="this" hx-swap="outerHTML"
        hx-headers='{"HX-Request": "true"}'>
    {# THE HIDDEN ANCHOR. Every value the card presents inside the derivation
       block travels back with the decision, so the POST validates against the
       EXACT computation he was looking at rather than recomputing "latest" --
       the GET/POST TOCTOU the hazard-2 gotcha forbids. #}
    {% for name, value in po.anchor_fields %}
    <input type="hidden" name="{{ name }}" value="{{ value }}">
    {% endfor %}
    {# THE RULING-3 CONTEXT ANCHOR, deliberately OUTSIDE the manifest loop: it
       is not part of the framework DERIVATION and must not enter
       `anchor_digest`. It carries the ledger row that GOVERNED this latch when
       the page rendered, so an A -> B -> A correction keys differently from the
       first answer and writes, while a double-click on one render still
       collapses. #}
    <input type="hidden" name="prior_intent_id"
           value="{{ row.prior_intent_id }}">
    <button type="submit" name="intent_kind" value="place"
            class="latch-intent-accept">ACCEPT - log this order</button>
    <button type="submit" name="intent_kind" value="decline"
            class="latch-intent-decline">DECLINE</button>
    <label>reason:
      <input type="text" name="decline_reason" maxlength="500">
    </label>
  </form>
  {% else %}
  <h3 class="latch-prepared-order-heading">PREPARED ORDER WITHHELD</h3>
  <p class="latch-prepared-order-withheld">{{ po.withheld_detail }}</p>
  {% endif %}
</section>
{% endif %}
{% if row.prompt_required or row.attest_correction_available %}
{# THE ATTESTATION PROMPT rides on the DISPOSITION, and on exactly one:
   `discipline_lapse`. The cell has ALREADY been scored as a lapse -- attesting
   is the operator's opportunity to CORRECT it, not a request for permission to
   score it. Prompting anywhere else (above all on a latch whose panel never
   presented a decision) trains the dismissal reflex, and dismissal is what
   eventually kills the honest answer on the cell that matters. #}
<section class="latch-attest-prompt{% if row.attest_correction_available %} latch-attest-correction{% endif %}">
  {% if row.attest_correction_available %}
  {# A CORRECTION AFFORDANCE, NOT A PROMPT. An attestation already governs this
     latch, and the ledger is append-only, so amending it means writing a NEW
     row whose answer supersedes -- which the operator can only do if a control
     exists. `was_away` and `chose_not_to_act` land in DIFFERENT R buckets, so
     an uncorrectable first answer is a measurement he cannot fix. It is worded
     and styled as an amendment rather than a question precisely because a
     recurring QUESTION on a settled cell is what trains the dismissal reflex. #}
  <p>Recorded: <strong>{{ row.attested_disposition }}</strong>. If that is not
     what happened, answer again - your LATEST answer governs and nothing is
     overwritten.</p>
  {% else %}
  <p>This mandate went terminal with no action recorded. If that is wrong, say
     what happened - the ledger will believe you.</p>
  {% endif %}
  <form hx-post="/latches/intent" hx-target="this" hx-swap="outerHTML"
        hx-headers='{"HX-Request": "true"}'>
    <input type="hidden" name="view_session_date" value="{{ vm.horizon_session }}">
    <input type="hidden" name="candidate_id" value="{{ row.candidate_id }}">
    <input type="hidden" name="intent_kind" value="attest">
    <input type="hidden" name="prior_intent_id"
           value="{{ row.prior_intent_id }}">
    {% for value, label in row.attest_options %}
    <label>
      {# REQUIRED for the same reason the validity group is: nothing is
         pre-selected, and a submit with no selection 400s over the only
         control. #}
      <input type="radio" name="attested_disposition" value="{{ value }}"
             required>
      {{ label }}
    </label>
    {% endfor %}
    {# THE EXACT-LINKAGE INPUT. `acted_manually` is the ONE path that exists for
       an order the framework did not prepare, and section G.4's exact linkage
       comes from a CAPTURED broker order id -- so without this field the
       distinguishability query can only ever INFER his manual orders from
       params, which is a heuristic. Optional: he may not have the id to hand,
       and refusing the attestation for want of it would lose the attestation. #}
    <label>broker order id (optional - makes the attribution EXACT rather than
      inferred):
      <input type="text" name="actual_broker_order_id" maxlength="64">
    </label>
    <button type="submit">Record this attestation</button>
  </form>
</section>
{% endif %}
